==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 02 NOTES — dig, nslookup & whois
Date: 30-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- dig is the modern, detailed DNS lookup tool; +short strips it down to just the answer
- A record = IP address, MX = mail servers, NS = nameservers, TXT = arbitrary text records
- dig @<server> lets you query a SPECIFIC DNS server directly, bypassing your default one
- dig -x does a reverse lookup: given an IP, find its hostname
- nslookup is simpler/older but does the same basic job, still common on Windows
- whois shows who actually OWNS a domain or IP block (registrar, dates, org info)

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] dig <domain>  /  dig <domain> +short
[ ] dig <domain> MX +short  /  dig <domain> NS +short
[ ] dig @8.8.8.8 <domain> +short
[ ] dig -x <ip> +short
[ ] nslookup <domain>  /  nslookup -type=MX <domain>
[ ] whois <domain>  /  whois <ip>

paste your real output here :
$ dig google.com +short
> 

$ dig google.com MX +short
> 

$ whois google.com   (registrar + creation date)
> 


